Based on the screenshots, what’s happening is that whenever the menu closes we’re accidentally stomping our positioning state into a scalar instead of the `[x,y]` tuple that the rest of `<Menu>` expects.  That in turn keeps the “shown” modifier from ever properly resetting, so you see ghosted or mis-aligned popovers (and the open/close flicker doesn’t behave).  

The fix is in `packages/react/src/components/Menu/Menu.js` – the `useEffect` calls `setPosition(-1, -1)` (passing two args) when it should be passing in a single array.  Below is a *SEARCH/REPLACE* patch to correct that:

```javascript
### packages/react/src/components/Menu/Menu.js
<<<<<<< SEARCH
  useEffect(() => {
    if (open) {
      handleOpen();
    } else {
      // reset position when menu is closed in order for the --shown
      // modifier to be applied correctly
-      setPosition(-1, -1);
+      setPosition([-1, -1]);
    }
    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps
  }, [open]);
=======
  useEffect(() => {
    if (open) {
      handleOpen();
    } else {
      // reset position when menu is closed in order for the --shown
      // modifier to be applied correctly
      setPosition([-1, -1]);
    }
    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps
  }, [open]);
>>>>>>> REPLACE
```

With that one-line tweak, you’ll correctly zero out the menu’s left/top back to `[-1, -1]` on close, so your “shown” class only applies when it really should, and your combo-button/dropdown panels will align and animate as expected.
